            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for splint, then the French for physiotherapist."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: éternuer",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "éternuer — \"to sneeze\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Au printemps, j'éternue tout le temps. In spring I sneeze all the time."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "À vos souhaits! — bless you! (said after a sneeze)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "éternuer is inherited, through Old French esternuer, from Latin sternūtāre \"to sneeze\" (EtymDB), built on sternuere (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
